libFuzzer harnesses for gnustep-base
====================================

These harnesses feed arbitrary bytes to the parsers and decoders that
commonly handle untrusted data:

  fuzz_plist       NSPropertyListSerialization, all formats (autodetected)
  fuzz_bplist      binary property lists only (input prefixed by "bplist00")
  fuzz_json        NSJSONSerialization
  fuzz_xml         GSSloppyXMLParser (GNUstep's own XML parser, also used
                   for XML property lists)
  fuzz_mime        GSMimeParser, whole document and in small chunks
  fuzz_keyed       NSKeyedUnarchiver
  fuzz_unarchiver  NSUnarchiver
  fuzz_portcoder   NSPortCoder (an incoming Distributed Objects message)
  fuzz_http        an HTTP response handled as NSURLProtocol does: GSMimeParser
                   in HTTP mode, NSHTTPURLResponse, redirects, cookies and
                   WWW-Authenticate challenges (GSHTTPAuthentication)
  fuzz_cookie      NSHTTPCookie Set-Cookie/Set-Cookie2 parsing
  fuzz_url         NSURL (absolute and relative) and NSURLComponents
  fuzz_uritemplate GSURITemplate (RFC 6570) parsing and expansion
  fuzz_predicate   NSPredicate/NSExpression format parsing
  fuzz_encoding    NSString conversion from and to every available encoding
  fuzz_base64      base64 and hexadecimal decoders, encode/decode round trips
  fuzz_tzif        NSTimeZone from TZif (tzfile) data
  fuzz_format      GSFormat (-initWithFormat:...) with arbitrary format strings
                   (see the harness for how the arguments are made safe)
  fuzz_scanner     NSScanner, a sequence of scan operations on a string
  fuzz_decimal     NSDecimalNumber/NSDecimal parsing, arithmetic, rounding
  fuzz_calendardate NSCalendarDate parsing and printing with calendar formats
  fuzz_regex       NSRegularExpression (GNUstep's code around ICU uregex)
  fuzz_charset     NSCharacterSet from bitmap data (with inputs of up to
                   40000 bytes, so that they cover more than the first plane)

An NSException raised for bad input is expected behaviour and is caught;
crashes, sanitizer reports, hangs and excessive memory use are bugs.
fuzz_base64 also aborts when an encode/decode round trip does not give
back the input.

Building and running
--------------------

The harnesses are built with gnustep-make, like any program using
gnustep-base: with the compiler and flags of the installed gnustep-make,
against the installed gnustep-base (headers and library) by default.
Install the version to be fuzzed first, or use fuzzlib=local to build
them against the library built in this source tree (after ./configure
and make at the top), with the headers in Headers/, as 'make check'
does.

When the compiler provides libFuzzer (clang), the harnesses are linked
with it.  Otherwise (gcc), they are linked with standalone.c, a simple
driver which understands the same main options: it runs the inputs
given on the command line, or fuzzes by random mutations of the corpus
files.  It has no coverage feedback, so it finds much less than
libFuzzer, but it still finds shallow bugs and replays inputs.

From the top of the source tree ('make' alone does not build them):

  make fuzz                    build, then fuzz each harness for 5 minutes
  make fuzz-replay             run each harness once over its corpus
  make fuzz-triage             one line per saved crash input

or in this directory:

  make                         build every harness (in obj/) and seedgen
  make fuzz                    fuzz each harness for 5 minutes in turn
  make replay                  run each harness once over its corpus
  make triage                  one line per saved crash input
  make clean                   remove obj/

Variables, given on the command line:

  fuzzobj=plist,json   only build and run these harnesses (names with or
                       without the fuzz_ prefix, separated by commas or
                       spaces); the default is all of them
  fuzztime=600         seconds to fuzz each harness (default 300)
  fuzzargs="-seed=1"   extra options for libFuzzer (or standalone.c)
  fuzzengine=standalone  link with standalone.c even if libFuzzer is
                       available
  asan=no              build without AddressSanitizer
  fuzzlib=local        link with the library built in ../Source and
                       compile with the headers in ../Headers, instead
                       of the installed ones (fuzzlib=installed, the
                       default)

Run 'make clean' after changing asan, fuzzengine or fuzzlib:
gnustep-make does not rebuild objects when only the flags change.

With fuzzlib=local the harnesses find the library through an RPATH
entry, which takes precedence over LD_LIBRARY_PATH, so they use the
build tree's library however they are started.

For example, from the top of the source tree:

  make fuzz fuzzobj=plist,bplist fuzztime=600

On its first run for a harness, 'make fuzz' fills corpus/ with the
seeds made by seedgen.  The corpus then grows in corpus/<harness>/;
crash, timeout and out-of-memory reproducers are stored in
crashes/<harness>/ and the libFuzzer output in logs/<harness>.log.
Fuzzing keeps going after a crash (libFuzzer -fork=1 -ignore_crashes=1;
standalone.c stops and run.sh starts it again);
'make fuzz' fails at the end if new reproducers were saved.  To
reproduce one:

  ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0 obj/fuzz_plist <file>

Leak detection is disabled because the runtime and some caches keep
objects alive for the life of the process.

Instrumenting the library
-------------------------

libFuzzer is guided by the code coverage of the program, and
AddressSanitizer only checks memory accesses in code compiled with it.
With an ordinary gnustep-base, only the harnesses themselves are
instrumented: libFuzzer then mutates almost blindly, and a bad access
inside the library is caught only when it goes through a function
AddressSanitizer intercepts (memcpy(), strlen(), free()...).  For real
fuzzing, use a gnustep-base compiled with instrumentation.  The
simplest is to build the source tree with it and use fuzzlib=local:

  ./configure
  make asan=yes ADDITIONAL_OBJCFLAGS=-fsanitize=fuzzer-no-link \
    ADDITIONAL_CFLAGS=-fsanitize=fuzzer-no-link
  make fuzz fuzzlib=local

('make clean' and 'make' again before using the tree for anything
else, such as 'make check' or 'make install').  Or install such a
library:

  ./configure
  make asan=yes ADDITIONAL_OBJCFLAGS=-fsanitize=fuzzer-no-link \
    ADDITIONAL_CFLAGS=-fsanitize=fuzzer-no-link
  make install

(with gcc, which has no libFuzzer, only 'make asan=yes').

Programs not built with these flags cannot use such a library, so
install it only in a GNUstep installation kept for fuzzing (a
container, or a separate installation prefix with its own GNUstep.sh).
